WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.968
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated writer

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:05.534
and comedian who is the
longest-serving "Weekend Update"

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:07.334
anchor in the history
of "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:10.133
which returns this week
with host Adam Driver

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.267
and musical guest
Olivia Rodrigo.

00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:14.501
His new scripted podcast,
"Trust Fall,"

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:16.267
a photo he was not
in the room...

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:19.801
[ Laughter ]
Like, how would that happen?

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:23.767
[ Laughter ]
Just be honest.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.534
[ Laughter ]
Just be honest.

00:00:26.534 --> 00:00:28.133
"Trust Fall" is available
on Audible.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:31.167
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend Colin Jost!

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:36.701
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:42.868
♪♪

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:49.000
♪♪

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.534 --> 00:00:53.801
-Thank you very much.
Thank you.

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:57.567
-You know, they're applauding me
for having the courage

00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:59.300
to call you out.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.067
What -- What looks fake?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.968
-Yeah.

00:01:03.968 --> 00:01:06.133
-Have you ever seen
me and Wanda together,

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.934
and we didn't look like this?
-Yeah, that's true.

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.567
You're almost always leaning.
She's taking a good selfie.

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.868
I say, now that I see you in
person, you do look like that.

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:15.534
-See?
-Um, uh, It's great.

00:01:15.534 --> 00:01:18.300
-It's not far off.
-I see you, uh -- It's my --

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.434
-You work -- We work together.
-We work together.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.601
-We were in the same building.
How do you get to work?

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:25.667
-How do I get to work?
The, uh, show, uh, sends a car.

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.000
I take a car to work.
How do you get to work?

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.367
-Uh, I bike.
I guess I'm just maybe

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:31.968
a little more man of the people.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:34.000
-You bike.
-I bike, I bike.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.167
-What do you do, a Citi Bike?
-No, I buy bikes.

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:40.300
And then I just throw them away
when I get here.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.033
[ Laughter ]
So I just, you know...

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:44.300
-Yes, man of the people.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.434
-You just, like,
leave them on the street?

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:48.033
Because there's not, like,
a bike trash can.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:50.334
-Yeah, well, I mean --
I, well, you know,

00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:53.167
it's a Citi Bike-like system.
-But it's just not Citi Bike.

00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:54.868
-But I just -- yeah,
but I just throw them away.

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.901
And I don't, you know --
I mean, it's --

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:59.868
I don't leave them in a shape
where they could be reused.

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:01.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.968
You know that big giant pile
of broken bikes

00:02:03.968 --> 00:02:05.534
that's right outside?
-That's your bike?

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:08.200
-That's my bikes.
Haven't you ever wondered?

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:09.934
You thought it was one of those
cool sculptures they put up

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:12.434
sometimes at 30 Rock.
-I did. I thought it was Koons.

00:02:12.434 --> 00:02:15.267
-Oh, I get Koons a lot.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:17.968
-That's great. That's your
favorite kind of bike?

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:19.567
The one you just do once?
-Yeah.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:20.968
The disposable bikes.
-Yeah.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:23.200
-I think I'm
an environmental guy, you know.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.300
You guys know that about me.
But I bike my bike.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.467
And I don't know
if you've noticed now --

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:28.234
I feel like,
in the last year --

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:29.701
maybe you guys
have noticed this, too --

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:32.133
that somehow
they've allowed pedicabs

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:35.801
to just blast the loudest music
you've ever heard.

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:38.234
[ Laughter ]
And for some reason,

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:41.701
I find that they're all
exclusively playing "Despacito."

00:02:41.701 --> 00:02:43.934
-Yeah.
-Like, it's like

00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:46.534
it's a very festive version
of hell, you know?

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:49.634
Like, that's all you hear.
And it's so loud,

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:50.934
and I will just be biking,
I'm like,

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:52.534
"Oh, what's approaching me
from behind?

00:02:52.534 --> 00:02:54.567
Is it the entire
Puerto Rican Day parade?"

00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:56.133
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:58.434
-No, it's a single
empty pedicab.

00:02:58.434 --> 00:02:59.667
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:02.000
-And I realized that all
the speakers project out.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.300
So if you're in
the actual pedicab,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.267
it's relatively quiet.
-Yeah.

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:07.100
You're, like, you think
you're on your way

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.334
to a club 20 blocks away
that's playing "Despacito."

00:03:09.334 --> 00:03:11.234
-Exactly. I mean, the dream.
-The dream.

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:12.934
-We all, you know...
-You're like, "Faster!"

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:14.901
-Yeah.
-Do you ever, in those moments,

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:17.501
like, when you see
an empty pedicab blasting music,

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:19.133
do you try to step in and go,
like, "I might --"

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:21.334
again, this isn't
my business per se,

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:24.234
but I might have a note,
"I feel like, by blasting this,

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:26.968
people don't want to be
in your pedicab."

00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:30.133
-Maybe -- I think it's aimed
at, like, a full family,

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:32.367
that the dad is so upset
at everyone

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:33.934
and he's like,
"Just get in the --

00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:35.267
I don't want to hear
any of you anymore.

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:38.100
I just need 'Despacito'
right now."

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.400
[ Laughter ]
I think that's

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.267
what "Despacito" means --
"Desperate Dad."

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:46.067
-They say, "'Despacito' times
call for 'Despacito' measures."

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:47.601
-That's right, that's right.
You know.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:50.501
-We know this famous saying.
-Famously.

00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:52.167
-You're back in the swing
at the show.

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:53.434
It's been lovely
to have you guys back.

00:03:53.434 --> 00:03:55.067
We missed a few
because of the writers' strike.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.868
And now it's been wonderful.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:56.868 --> 00:03:58.734
-It's great.
It's been very nice.

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:00.801
-Love the new cast.
Love the young cast.

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:03.067
-I love the new cast.
They're great.

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:05.367
The last couple years
have been really great.

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:07.434
They're all fun.
I love hanging out with them.

00:04:07.434 --> 00:04:09.667
They're doing great on the show.

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.234
And it's very nice to be back.

00:04:12.234 --> 00:04:14.801
We got Adam Driver
who's, like, fantastic.

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:19.200
Really, really very good
at the show.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.968
-I'm happy about the outcome
for the real person,

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:25.934
but Bowen as George Santos
was a real gift to the show.

00:04:25.934 --> 00:04:28.200
-That was so --
and to all of America, really.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.400
-All of America.
And you know,

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.734
maybe not the most obvious
casting in the beginning,

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.167
but you guys worked
on this together.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:35.934
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:38.300
And he -- You know,
Bowen is fantastic.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.300
And I feel like I don't think
it's necessarily goodbye

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.267
to George Santos.
I feel like it's, "Hello,

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:46.100
a thousand documentaries
about him," is my guess.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.234
I feel like
we'll probably see him.

00:04:47.234 --> 00:04:48.868
Like, there's almost no chance
he's not

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:51.634
in a Super Bowl commercial
for, like, pistachios.

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:54.868
-Yeah,
and someone's like, "George!"

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:57.167
That definitely is a line.
-And he's like, "What?"

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:58.801
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:02.367
-Pistachios.
-I'm Mr. Pistachio.

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:03.567
-[ Chuckles ]

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:06.000
And then he also, like --
I do feel like

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.067
what I thought about
George Santos, too,

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:11.501
is that almost, like, we should
give him more credit

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:14.234
because he was able
to get elected,

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:16.634
but he only -- he used all
the campaign stuff on,

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:19.467
like, Botox and, you know,
like Hermès.

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:22.567
Like, these other people
spent it all on the campaign

00:05:22.567 --> 00:05:24.334
and couldn't win.
-Yeah.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:26.300
-Isn't that kind of impressive?
-Well, of course you won.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.100
You con those people
out of their money,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.400
and you used it
to run against us.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.234
He's like, "No."
-"No."

00:05:31.234 --> 00:05:33.834
Would you have rather --
wouldn't you rather him

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:36.934
look like real, like,
you know, sauced up

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:39.567
instead of having to see, like,
another campaign ad from him?

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:42.400
It's cool, you know?
-It is very -- Again,

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.801
the best thing about him is
the lies didn't seem impressive.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:47.100
Like, he lied about things
that you wouldn't think

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.200
another person would vote
for you if you were like,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.634
"Oh, he played
college volleyball.

00:05:50.634 --> 00:05:52.267
He's my guy."
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:52.267 --> 00:05:54.868
Yeah, I never thought
I would try --

00:05:54.868 --> 00:05:56.634
that someone would try
to impress me

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:59.434
with college volleyball.
-And Santos did, and it worked.

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:00.901
-And that's
why he's a visionary.

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:02.634
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.634 --> 00:06:05.167
-He also does make --
I feel like he made people

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:06.934
feel better
about themselves, too.

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:08.868
Like, you know,
when you saw that going on,

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.033
you're like, "Oh,
I'm not that bad of a person."

00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:12.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:14.467
-Adam Driver
was in "Marriage Story"

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:16.033
with your wife,
Scarlett Johansson.

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:17.734
-That's right.
-Is that a weird thing?

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:19.200
[ Cheers and applause ]
Give it up for "Marriage Story."

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.834
-Yeah, great movie.
-Because that was, like,

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.200
maybe one of the best movies
ever made

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.100
about the dissolution
of a marriage.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.234
-Yes, I guess it is --
It's odd to meet someone

00:06:27.234 --> 00:06:29.734
who played a person
who divorced your wife.

00:06:29.734 --> 00:06:32.033
That's a weird -- 'Cause I guess
when you date an actor,

00:06:32.033 --> 00:06:33.667
I've heard, you know,
they sometimes

00:06:33.667 --> 00:06:35.634
have to kiss people in movies
or whatever.

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:37.834
-You found out, though,
after you said "I do," right?

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:39.934
-The minute after.
-Yeah.

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.734
She's like,
"Now that it's official,

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.767
I have some things
to tell you about acting."

00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:46.300
-She's like,
"Look at this list!"

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.834
I'm kidding.
I don't keep a list.

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:50.601
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.601 --> 00:06:55.634
Uh, yeah, it is weird to meet
someone that that was played --

00:06:55.634 --> 00:06:57.334
because you're like, "Oh, I was
in a movie with your wife."

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:59.601
"Oh, what did you do?"
"I divorced her."

00:06:59.601 --> 00:07:02.300
And by the way, I should have
said "spoiler alert,"

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.534
but "Marriage Story" is
actually about divorce.

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:05.968
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.968 --> 00:07:08.033
-So if you thought like, "Oh,
it's a 'Father of the Bride'..."

00:07:08.033 --> 00:07:10.167
-You kind of know right away.
-Yeah, right.

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:11.868
They get that out really fast.
-Yeah.

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:13.467
Like, pretty much, like --
-The first line is,

00:07:13.467 --> 00:07:15.968
"What are you doing?!
I hate you!"

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:19.634
-Um, we saw each other
at, uh,

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:21.634
the Natural History Museum gala.
-Oh, yes.

00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:24.100
Seth emceed the gala
and was fantastic.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.968
-Thank you.
-Great job. Excellent work.

00:07:26.968 --> 00:07:30.133
-You sort of went --
You sort of went viral

00:07:30.133 --> 00:07:32.601
for a red-carpet moment.
-Did I? Oh, wow.

00:07:32.601 --> 00:07:34.534
-I want to show it first.
And then I want you

00:07:34.534 --> 00:07:36.434
to explain what happened.
-Okay, okay.

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:37.934
Here's a...
-Okay, okay.

00:07:37.934 --> 00:07:41.534
[ Indistinct conversations ]

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:44.601
[ Camera shutters clicking ]

00:07:44.601 --> 00:07:47.834
[ Indistinct conversations
continue ]

00:07:47.834 --> 00:07:50.534
[ Laughter ]

00:07:50.534 --> 00:07:54.133
-So, what happened?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:56.534 --> 00:07:57.801
-So, I don't understand.

00:07:57.801 --> 00:08:00.334
Are you implying
that someone gave me

00:08:00.334 --> 00:08:04.000
some sort of gummy mushroom
right before

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.801
I went to the red carpet?
Is that what you're implying?

00:08:06.801 --> 00:08:10.200
-And that you froze like a deer
in oncoming traffic,

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.968
to the degree that your wife
had to lean over

00:08:12.968 --> 00:08:16.267
in front of a camera crew
and be like, "Are you okay?"

00:08:16.267 --> 00:08:18.400
[ Laughter ]

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.300
-Are you implying that
when a thousand flashbulbs

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.868
went off in front of my face,

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:26.267
that I thought I was staring
into the dawn of the cosmos...

00:08:26.267 --> 00:08:28.868
[ Laughter ]
...and that I saw,

00:08:28.868 --> 00:08:32.767
fully realized, my entire past
and future in one moment,

00:08:32.767 --> 00:08:36.968
until my wife had to rip me
out of the space-time continuum

00:08:36.968 --> 00:08:40.367
and say, "Are you okay?"
[ Laughter ]

00:08:40.367 --> 00:08:42.567
I don't know
what you're implying, but...

00:08:42.567 --> 00:08:44.133
-Can I tell you something?
-Yeah.

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:45.934
-But I've seen that face before.
-Right.

00:08:45.934 --> 00:08:48.167
-Because we work together,
and I want to show it

00:08:48.167 --> 00:08:49.534
one more time
and let everybody here know

00:08:49.534 --> 00:08:51.767
this is the face Colin
would make if I'd be like,

00:08:51.767 --> 00:08:54.300
"Hey, did you finish
writing the cold open?"

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.567
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.567 --> 00:08:58.067
-[ Chuckles ]

00:08:58.067 --> 00:09:01.267
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.701 --> 00:09:08.701
[ Applause ]

00:09:08.701 --> 00:09:11.133
-We'll be right back
with more Colin after this!

